        "For every x,v, alpha*||v||^2 <= D(DV)(x)[v][v] and D(DV)(x)[v][v] <= beta*||v||^2",
        "0 < eta",
        "beta*eta <= 1"
      ],
      "conclusion": "All displayed probability, kernel, conditional law, marginal, operator contraction, selfadjointness, mean preservation, positive-defect, positive-root, full-energy and alternative-root uniqueness properties are produced under exactly the displayed hypotheses.",
      "scopes": "For every y, equality S(y)=tilted(volume, log_weight_y) is equality of measures at every state, including states outside any nu-full set; its normalizing constant may depend on y. In contrast, the integral action formula for T is stated separately for each observable u as equality nu-almost everywhere, allowing the exceptional set to depend on u. It does not claim one common exceptional set for all observables, nor pointwise action at every y for arbitrary representatives. Norm, mean and energy statements quantify over every L2 equivalence class. Lambda.IsCondKernel S describes disintegration of the second coordinate given the first: Lambda is obtained from its first marginal nu and S; both marginals are nu. Products A*B mean composition, so T*T is T squared, I is the identity bounded operator and the defect is I-T^2. Since T is selfadjoint, its displayed defect/energy relation has the form associated with I-T* T, but the exact statement uses T*T and supplies selfadjointness separately. Gamma and Gamma' are bounded positive operators with the identical square D. Only Gamma is given the energy certificate. Alternative-root uniqueness requires only positivity and exact square for Gamma', with conclusion equality of operators.",
      "constant_dependencies": "The literal constants are alpha,beta,eta with alpha>0, alpha<=beta and 0<eta<=1/beta through the displayed cap; the Gaussian amplitude is sqrt(eta), the reflection coefficient is 2, the midpoint coefficient is 1/2 and the kernel quadratic denominator is 8*eta. No extra higher-derivative bound, restriction on dim(E), numerical convergence rate or computational cost is supplied.",
